use std
into pfont.amni

This file is a module with no main (namespace pfont). It turns PDF font dictionaries into what text extraction needs: how many bytes each character code takes, the Unicode text of each code and its advance width. Fonts are loaded once per font dict node and kept by index. Every module-level name starts with `fo_` or `FO_`. All loops over file data are bounded by the data length; nothing here may crash on a malformed font. PDF objects are read only through these `pdf` functions, which all take node indexes and dereference them: `pdf::pf_kind(n)` (a `pdf::PF_` type constant such as `pdf::PF_DICT`, `pdf::PF_STREAM`, `pdf::PF_ARR`, `pdf::PF_NAME`, `pdf::PF_INT`, `pdf::PF_REAL`, or -1), `pdf::pf_dget(n, key)` (the value node of a dict or stream for key, a name written without its slash, or -1), `pdf::pf_dhas(n, key)`, `pdf::pf_dict_of(n)`, `pdf::pf_bytes(n)` (the bytes of a name or string node), `pdf::pf_name_is(n, v)`, `pdf::pf_int(n, default)`, `pdf::pf_real(n, default)`, `pdf::pf_len(n)` and `pdf::pf_at(n, i)` (array length and element), `pdf::pf_decode(n, out) bool` (a stream's decoded data) and `pdf::pf_text(n, out)` (a text string as UTF-8); `pdf::pf_get(num)` takes an object number, not a node, and is not needed here.

```amni
import pdf;
import glyph;
import dm;
```

`struct FoFont` has `node int`, `kind int` (0 simple, 1 composite Type0, 2 Type3), `bold bool`, `italic bool`, `vertical bool`, `nb int` (fixed code length in bytes: 1 for simple fonts, 2 for Identity and UCS-2 CMaps, 0 when the code space ranges decide), `sjis int` (0, or a lead-byte rule for predefined multi-byte CMaps: 1 Shift-JIS, 2 EUC or GBK or Big5 or KSC style), `ucs2 bool` (codes are UTF-16BE Unicode), `ident bool` (code equals CID), `cs_at int`, `cs_n int`, `t_at int` (its 256 simple-font text entries in `fo_t_at` and `fo_t_len`, -1 for composite fonts), `m_at int`, `m_n int` (its ToUnicode entries), `c_at int`, `c_n int` (its code to CID entries), `g_at int`, `g_n int` (its CID to Unicode entries recovered from an embedded TrueType font), `w_first int`, `w_at int`, `w_n int` (simple widths in `fo_w`), `missing real`, `dw real` (composite default width), `wl_at int`, `wl_n int` (composite width ranges), `scale real` (glyph units to text space: 0.001, or for Type3 the FontMatrix's first number), `name_at int`, `name_len int` (BaseFont without any subset prefix, in `fo_names`).

Module state: `var fo_fonts FoFont[>..]`, `var fo_by_node int[>..]` (font index plus 1 by node index, 0 when not loaded), `var fo_names byte[>..]`, `var fo_t_at int[>..]`, `var fo_t_len int[>..]`, `var fo_txt byte[>..]` (UTF-8 text pool), `var fo_cs_lo int[>..]`, `var fo_cs_hi int[>..]`, `var fo_cs_nb int[>..]` (code space ranges), `var fo_m_lo int[>..]`, `var fo_m_hi int[>..]`, `var fo_m_at int[>..]`, `var fo_m_len int[>..]`, `var fo_m_step int[>..]` (ToUnicode entries: for codes lo to hi the text is the UTF-16 code units `fo_u16[at .. at + len]` with the last unit increased by (code minus lo) when step is 1, or unchanged when step is 0), `var fo_u16 int[>..]`, `var fo_c_lo int[>..]`, `var fo_c_hi int[>..]`, `var fo_c_cid int[>..]`, `var fo_g_cid int[>..]`, `var fo_g_cp int[>..]`, `var fo_w real[>..]`, `var fo_wl_lo int[>..]`, `var fo_wl_hi int[>..]`, `var fo_wl_w real[>..]`, `var fo_unmapped = 0` (count of codes shown with no text), `var fo_buf byte[>..]`, `var fo_tmp byte[>..]`.

`fo_reset()` clears all module state.

`fo_strip_subset(name const byte[:]) const byte[:]` returns name without a leading subset tag of exactly six upper-case ASCII letters and a plus sign.

`fo_has(s const byte[:], word const byte[:]) bool` is true when word occurs in s (case-sensitive).

`fo_put_utf16(units const int[:], out byte[>..]&)` appends UTF-16 code units as UTF-8, joining a valid high and low surrogate pair into one code point, turning a lone surrogate into U+FFFD and dropping code units 0.

`fo_hex_val(s const byte[:]) int` is the big-endian value of a byte string, and `fo_cmap_parse(data const byte[:], f int, to_unicode bool)` reads a CMap program (a ToUnicode stream or an embedded encoding CMap) into font f's entries. Tokenize with `pdf::pf_skip`, `pdf::pf_hex_string`, `pdf::pf_name` and plain number and keyword reading over data (not the object arena). Sections: "begincodespacerange" pairs of hex strings up to "endcodespacerange" add code space ranges (low value, high value, byte length of the low string) to `fo_cs_*`; "beginbfchar" pairs of a source hex string and a destination (a hex string of UTF-16 units, or a name, which goes through `glyph::gl_name_to_text` and is stored as the code units of its UTF-8 text decoded back to code points) up to "endbfchar" add entries with step 0; "beginbfrange" triples of low, high and either a hex string (step 1) or an array of hex strings (one step-0 entry per code from low upward, stopping at high or the end of the array) up to "endbfrange"; "begincidrange" triples low, high, first CID and "begincidchar" pairs code, CID add `fo_c_*` entries (a cidchar is a range of one code); "usecmap" after a name containing "Identity" sets the font's `ident` and `nb` 2. Ranges whose high is below low, or that span more than 65536 codes, are skipped; at most 200000 entries are added per font. When to_unicode is true the bf entries go to the font's `m_*` list, otherwise they are ignored. Keep each list's entries contiguous for the font by appending them at the end of the module lists and recording at and n on the font. After reading, sort the font's ToUnicode entries by low code with a stable sort.

`fo_tounicode(f int, code int, out byte[>..]&) bool` appends the ToUnicode text of code and returns true when one of font f's entries covers it: binary-search the last entry whose low is at most code, then step back through earlier entries (at most 16) until one whose high is at least code is found; the entry's units go through `fo_put_utf16`, with the last unit increased by (code minus low) when step is 1.

`fo_symbol_cp(code int) int` maps the Symbol font's letters: codes 65 to 90 map through the string "ΑΒΧΔΕΦΓΗΙϑΚΛΜΝΟΠΘΡΣΤΥςΩΞΨΖ" (one code point per letter, in order) and 97 to 122 through "αβχδεφγηιϕκλμνοπθρστυϖωξψζ"; codes 32 to 64 and 91 to 96 and 123 to 126 map to themselves except 34 (U+2200), 36 (U+2203), 39 (U+220B), 42 (U+2217), 45 (U+2212), 64 (U+2245), 92 (U+2234), 94 (U+22A5); 0xB7 is U+2022, 0xB1 U+00B1, 0xB4 U+00D7, 0xB8 U+00F7, 0xA3 U+2264, 0xB3 U+2265, 0xB9 U+2260, 0xBB U+2248, 0xA5 U+221E, 0xD6 U+221A, 0xE5 U+2211, 0xF2 U+222B, 0xAE U+2192, 0xAC U+2190, 0xDE U+21D2, 0xB6 U+2202, 0xD1 U+2207, 0xCE U+2208; everything else 0.

`fo_dingbat_cp(code int) int` maps ZapfDingbats: 51 is U+2713, 52 U+2714, 53 U+2715, 54 U+2716, 55 U+2717, 56 U+2718, 108 U+25CF, 110 U+25A0, 113 U+25A1, 115 U+25B2, 116 U+25BC, 117 U+25C6, 118 U+2756, 32 is a space, and any other code from 33 to 254 is U+2022.

`fo_base_cp(enc int, code int) int` is the code point of code under a base encoding: enc 1 StandardEncoding (`glyph::gl_std`), 2 WinAnsiEncoding (codes 128 to 159 through `dm::dm_cp1252`, codes 32 to 126 and 160 to 255 as Latin-1, code 173 as a hyphen, others 0), 3 MacRomanEncoding (`dm::dm_macroman` for 128 to 255, ASCII below), 4 Symbol (`fo_symbol_cp`), 5 ZapfDingbats (`fo_dingbat_cp`), 6 MacExpertEncoding (treat as StandardEncoding).

`fo_builtin_type1(f int, data const byte[:], names_out byte[>..]&, codes_out int[>..]&) bool` reads the clear-text part of an embedded Type 1 font program (up to the first "eexec", or all of it when absent): when it contains "/Encoding StandardEncoding" return false; otherwise collect every "dup <int> /<name> put" as a code and glyph name (names appended to names_out separated by a 0 byte, codes in order to codes_out) and return true when at least one was found.

`fo_ttf_cmap(data const byte[:], cps int[>..]&)` reads the 'cmap' table of a TrueType or OpenType font program and fills cps so that cps[gid] is the Unicode code point of glyph gid (0 when unknown, keeping the smallest code point when several map to one glyph; cps is sized to the largest glyph id seen plus 1, capped at 65536): read the table directory (offset 4 holds numTables as a big-endian u16, entries of 16 bytes start at 12: tag, checksum, offset, length), find 'cmap', prefer subtable platform 3 encoding 10 (format 12), then 3 1 (format 4), then platform 0 (format 4 or 12); format 4: segCountX2, then endCode, a reserved u16, startCode, idDelta and idRangeOffset arrays; for each segment and each code from start to end (skip 0xFFFF), the glyph is (code plus idDelta) mod 65536 when idRangeOffset is 0, else the u16 at the idRangeOffset entry's own position plus idRangeOffset plus 2 × (code minus start), and when that is nonzero plus idDelta mod 65536; format 12: groups of start code, end code and start glyph (u32 each, after a 16-byte header) with at most 200000 codes in total. Every read is bounds checked; a bad table leaves cps empty.

`fo_load(node int) int` returns the font index for font dict node (after `pdf::pf_deref`), loading it on first use; -1 when node is not a dict. Loading:
- Name: /BaseFont through `fo_strip_subset`; bold when the name contains "Bold", "Black", "Heavy", "Semibold", "SemiBold" or "Demi", or the descriptor's /FontWeight is at least 600, or its /Flags has bit value 262144; italic when the name contains "Italic" or "Oblique" or the descriptor's /ItalicAngle is nonzero or /Flags has bit value 64. The descriptor is /FontDescriptor, or for Type0 the descendant font's /FontDescriptor.
- Type0 (`/Subtype /Type0`): kind 1, `t_at` -1. The descendant is the first element of /DescendantFonts. /Encoding as a name: names containing "Identity" set `ident`, `nb` 2 and `vertical` when it ends with "-V"; names containing "UCS2" or "UTF16" set `ucs2` and `nb` 2; names containing "RKSJ" set `sjis` 1; names containing "EUC", "GBK", "GBpc", "GBT", "KSC", "B5", "ETen", "HKscs" or "UHC" set `sjis` 2; any other name sets `nb` 2; names ending "-V" are vertical. /Encoding as a stream: decode it with `pdf::pf_decode` and run `fo_cmap_parse` with to_unicode false; `nb` stays 0 unless set by usecmap, and when no code space ranges were found use `nb` 2. Widths: /DW of the descendant (default 1000) and its /W array, whose items are either "c [w1 w2 ...]" (CIDs c, c+1, ... get those widths) or "c1 c2 w"; store each as a range (lo, hi, width) in `fo_wl_*` (an array form adds one range per width). When there is no ToUnicode and the descendant is /CIDFontType2 with a /FontFile2 stream in its descriptor, decode the font program and use `fo_ttf_cmap`; then map CIDs to glyph ids through /CIDToGIDMap (a stream of big-endian u16 glyph ids indexed by CID, or the name Identity or absent meaning gid = cid) and record (cid, code point) pairs with a nonzero code point in `fo_g_*`, sorted by CID, for every CID up to 65535 that the map covers (for Identity, every glyph id with a code point).
- Type3: kind 2, `scale` is the first number of /FontMatrix (default 0.001); otherwise like a simple font.
- Simple fonts (Type1, MMType1, TrueType, Type3, anything else): kind 0 (or 2), `nb` 1, `scale` 0.001 for non-Type3. Widths: /FirstChar (default 0) and /Widths into `fo_w`; `missing` is the descriptor's /MissingWidth (default 0). Text: build the font's 256 text entries: choose a base encoding number: /Encoding as a name gives it directly (StandardEncoding 1, WinAnsiEncoding 2, MacRomanEncoding 3, MacExpertEncoding 6); /Encoding as a dict uses its /BaseEncoding when present; when no base is given, use the embedded Type1 font's built-in encoding when `fo_builtin_type1` finds one in the descriptor's /FontFile (decoded with `pdf::pf_decode`), else 4 when the base name contains "Symbol", 5 when it contains "Dingbats", else 2 for TrueType fonts, else 1. For each code 0 to 255 the default text is the base encoding's code point (`fo_base_cp`), or for a built-in encoding the code point of its glyph name through `glyph::gl_name_to_text` (codes the built-in encoding does not list fall back to StandardEncoding). Then the /Differences array of an /Encoding dict (an int sets the current code, each following name is assigned to the current code which then increases by one) replaces those codes' text with `glyph::gl_name_to_text` of the name, leaving the text empty when the name is not recognised. Store each code's UTF-8 text in `fo_txt` with its start and length in `fo_t_at` and `fo_t_len`.
- ToUnicode (for any kind): when the font dict has a /ToUnicode stream, decode it with `pdf::pf_decode` and run `fo_cmap_parse` with to_unicode true.

`fo_next(f int, s const byte[:], at int&) int` reads the next character code of font f from s at at (at is advanced; returns -1 at the end): simple fonts take one byte; composite fonts with `nb` 2 take two bytes big endian (a single trailing byte is taken alone); `sjis` 1 takes two bytes when the first is 0x81 to 0x9F or 0xE0 to 0xFC, else one; `sjis` 2 takes two bytes when the first is at least 0x81, else one; otherwise, with code space ranges, try lengths 1 to 4 and take the first length n for which the n-byte big-endian value lies inside a range of byte length n; when none matches take one byte.

`fo_cid(f int, code int) int` is the CID of a composite font's code: `ident` or `ucs2` gives code; otherwise the code to CID entries (the last matching range wins: cid is first CID plus code minus low); when nothing matches, code.

`fo_text(f int, code int, nbytes int, out byte[>..]&) bool` appends the Unicode text of code to out and returns whether any was found: first `fo_tounicode`; a ToUnicode result that is only U+0000 or empty does not count; then for simple fonts the code's text entry; for composite fonts with `ucs2` the code as a UTF-16 unit (or two bytes forming a pair across consecutive codes is out of scope; a surrogate gives U+FFFD); then the `fo_g_*` entry for `fo_cid` (binary search); otherwise false. Count a miss in `fo_unmapped` when nothing is found and the code is not 0.

`fo_width(f int, code int) real` is the horizontal advance of code in text space units for a font size of 1: simple fonts use `fo_w[w_at + code - w_first]` when the code is in range and that width is above 0, else `missing` when above 0, else `glyph::gl_base_width` of the font name and code; composite fonts use the last width range covering `fo_cid` of the code, else `dw`; Type3 multiplies by `scale` instead of 0.001; vertical fonts still return this horizontal width.

`fo_is_bold(f int) bool`, `fo_is_italic(f int) bool`, `fo_is_vertical(f int) bool`, `fo_kind(f int) int` and `fo_name(f int) const byte[:]` read the font's fields.
